From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
Date: Fri, 11 Mar 1994 09:10:51 -0800
Subject: [#94-3-136] BIO: Codons and amino acids

In my recent posting about mitochondrial DNA coding, I should perhaps have
elaborated that non-bacterial living cells are internally compartmentalized;
they consist of 10-micron-diameter balloons of stuff, part of whose contents
is different kinds of smaller balloons of different stuff.  The smaller
balloons (well, they're not all balloon-shaped) are called _organelles_,
analogously with the _organs_ that make up many multi-celled organisms.
Mitochondria are one kind of organelle; they are involved in the final stages
of cellular metabolism; that is, in the production of ATP by chemical
processing of food.  Chloroplasts are another kind of organelle; they do
photosynthesis, e.g., in plants.
                                         -- Jay Freeman

