 > (which apparently is what PCR is beyond it's role as a prescription
 > for rationality). You are certain until something causes you to be
 > uncertain.
 
I would be reluctant to characterize evolutionary epistemology (the 
epistemological aspect of PCR, if you like) as a "coherency 
epistemology". Coherency epistemologies follow the same tired 
justificationist rut that old-fashioned rationalism and naive empiricism do.
 
As far as telling me it "does not follow", I was simply restating what 
you had already said about "rearranging the package" in the passage of 
yours that I had quoted, which you seem to have forgotten you ever wrote.
 
 > And I still contend that doubt disables action and dissipates the
 > constructive, focused use of your energy that naturally flows from
 > certainty.
 
Okay. Maybe it does for you. I'm sorry. But doubt does not disable action 
for me nor for hundreds of people I have met. And your discussion of 
"energy that flows from certainty" sounds frankly superstitious. Doubt 
about ones wants and intentions is debilitating, and certainty about them 
enables action. But for the most part we are discussing a paradigm of 
certainty about the outside world.
 
 > Eric again: <Relativism does not necessarily mean, as you seem to
