 > distinguish between good ideas and bad ones. It does not
 > necessarily mean that we cannot decide or that we cannot act.>
 > 
 > Then it is not relativism, because this is exactly what relativism
 > means. Relativism is Greek skepticism and produces a decline in any
 > extropic civilization as it did to Greece.
 
Well, then I misunderstood you. In that case, my argument is certainly 
*not* relativistic, since relativism contends that certainty is 
impossible, and if certainty were impossible, than all levels of 
certainty (zero, that is) would be equal, and one would be neither more 
nor less certain about one given claim than about any other claim. This 
is the exact negation of my claim that certainty is relative, that one 
can be more or less certain about a given claim than about another claim 
compared to it.
 
 > Placing Popper and Bartley on the pedastal of "authority" 
 
I was not placing Popper and Bartley on a pedestal of authority. Not once 
have I referred to a specific argument of Popper's or a specific argument 
of Bartley's and expected anyone to accept it simply because it belonged 
to Popper or Bartley. That would be setting them on a pedestal. Likewise, 
I am not going to pay much attention to an argument that is presented to 
me which I am asked to accept, or at least treat seriously, on the simple 
ground that it once belonged to Socrates or Mussolini or Nietzsche.
 
You and Tim Starr seem to be interested in understanding PCR and
evolutionary epistemology. I'm interested in discovering whether and how
your absolutist epistemological ideas are compatible with Extropian ideas
in general. I am particularly interested in trying to find the connections
between Hoppe-style applications of praxeology to values (on the one hand)
and Extropian ideas (on the other), if any such connections exist. The
bandwidth is limited. Can we please leave the biographical details to one
side? 

