I'm sure you're not the only one who's unfamiliar with them.  Genus and
differentia are the two parts of a definition.  Genus is the general
category to which a thing belongs, and differentia are the things which
make it stand out from that category.  In other words, the genus is the
class of things it's most similar to, and differentia is how it most
significantly differs from them.  Exemplia gratia: man is defined as
a rational animal.  Animal is the genus.  Man is an animal a being with
the power of locomotion.  The differentia is that man's not just any
kind of animal, man's rational, not only a being with the power of
locomotion but also one with the power of reason.  Another example is
the definition of kindness: a disposition to do good.  Genus: disposi-
tion.  Differentia: to do good.

This kind of definition is also sometimes called "analytic definition."
 
