On Fri, 11 Mar 1994, Chris Phoenix wrote:

> week, but so far it sounds fascinating.  Can you give me a brief
> description, or pointers to references, or the email address of
> someone who doesn't mind explaining it to a newbie?

The primary reference is THE RETREAT TO COMMITMENT by William W. Bartley 
III. I believe it's published by Open Court. You might try local 
libraries (especially academic ones) or large academically-oriented used 
bookstores. I found my copy in a used bookstore three blocks from UC 
Berkeley. Since then, a collection of essays called EVOLUTIONARY 
EPISTEMOLOGY, edited by Bartley, Sir Karl Popper, someone named 
Radnitsky, and someone whose name I forget has appeared, and this also 
looks as if it might be a good sourcebook.

The two major defenders of it that I've seen on the list have been me at 
FutureNerd Steve Witham AKA fnerd. I don't remember his email address off 
the top of my head. I know that Max More has been studying Bartley's book 
recently and is also interested in PCR. The first mention of it that I 
know among extropians was in the interview of Mark Miller by David 
Krieger in issue #11 of EXTROPY.

PCR stands for Pancritical Rationalism. Basically it is an *answer* to 
(not a reformulation of) ancient Greek Skepticism. The Skeptics correctly 
pointed out that no idea could be justified except in terms of some other 
idea, and that ultimately this process of justification had to come to an 
end in some unjustified idea or set of ideas. Hence, one could believe 
anything one wants. This is a problem which has plague all epistemology 
for centuries, including the recent ones.

Bartley takes some cues from Popper's philosophy of science and decides 
that the problem lies with justificationism itself. Bartley rejects 
justificationism, and prescribes a model of rationality called 
Pancritical Rationalism, in which we recognize that we distinguish 
between good/true ideas and bad/false ones by seeing how well they stand 
up to criticisms such as logical inconsistency, conflict with 
experimental data, etc and *not* by their justification in terms of other 
"more basic" ideas. This is a generalization of Popper's "falsifiability" 
criterion.

